items.body:
Adds `tasks/join-copper-to-galvanised-pipe/`. A pipe run lies on the bench: an old galvanised steel pipe, a gap, a new copper section, a second gap, and an existing copper pipe. A tray holds three fittings: a galvanised steel coupling, a copper coupling and a dielectric union (brass half, black insulating ring, galvanised half). The instruction only asks for a fitting from the tray to be seated in each gap and released. It doesn't mention metals, corrosion or unions. Joining copper straight to galvanised steel with a plain metal coupling sets up galvanic corrosion, so the steel-to-copper gap needs the union, with its brass end toward the copper. The copper-to-copper gap takes the plain copper coupling. There is only one union, so "always use the special part" fails, and the fitting nearest each gap is the wrong one for it.

The scripted demo scored **1.0** in both reset-check trials and in the recorded trial. Dream-1 scored **0.0 in 1,200 steps**: it reached over the tray several times but never seated a fitting. Shortcut checks in the simulator:
- the union seated backwards (brass end toward the steel) scores 0;
- the nearest fitting in each gap (steel coupling in the steel-to-copper gap) scores 0;
- the union in the copper-to-copper gap scores 0;
- only the union joint done scores 0.75; only the copper joint done scores 0.25;
- both fittings right but never released scores 0;
- doing nothing scores 0.

A wrong fitting in either gap zeroes the score, and knocking the pipe run is a latched 0. 62 offline tests pass (`python -m pytest -q test_scoring.py test_task.py`). The README has the layout, run commands, scoring and results.

Media **M-RC2HUT** includes the Dream-1 run, the full-score demo and the setup screenshot.
